e I already have a Dots Chow and B1, B2, B5, B8. If I focus on Dots, maybe keep B1, B2, B5, B8 and discard other suit tiles.  \n- But I also have many W tiles: W1, W2, W3, W6, W8, W9 \u2014 could try for a W suit hand if I draw more W\u2019s.  \nGiven I have only one meld and many singles, discarding a tile from a suit where I have few tiles might be better to reduce suit spread.  \n\nLooking at unshown tiles:  \nW1 (2 left), W2 (4), W3 (3), W6 (4), W8 (4), W9 (3) \u2014 so W tiles are relatively available.  \nB1 (4), B2 (2), B5 (3), B8 (2) \u2014 B8 only 2 left, B2 only 2 left, so maybe B8 or B2 is riskier.  \n\nSafest seems to be **W6** (middle tile, 4 left, no immediate adjacency to opponents\u2019 melds, W suit not heavily collected by others based on melds).  \n\n**Reason**: W6 is a middle tile with 4 copies left, not adjacent to opponents\u2019 visible Chows (previous player has W3-W5 but that\u2019s not directly connecting to W6 for a sequence unless they have W5-W7, but they discarded W1 and W4, not W5 or W7). Next player has no W melds. Across player has no melds. So W6 appears relatively safe. Discarding it keeps my Dots potential and possibly allows me to draw into a better hand structure.  \n\n**Answer**: Play W6"}
